From: Ubuntu Rockchip Builder <builder@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: rkvenc: encode H.264 frames on the VEPU580

The skeleton registered the block and advertised NV12 in, H.264 out, but
device_run returned every buffer with an error. Fill in the per-frame path
so the encoder actually produces a stream.

The block writes slice data and nothing else, so the driver composes the
sequence and picture parameter sets itself. That split is why the bitstream
writer and the parameter set builder take a plain description rather than
the driver's context: both can be checked against a real H.264 parser on a
host, which is how the Exp-Golomb coding and the emulation-prevention bytes
were verified before any of it reached the board.

Everything the decoder reads out of a parameter set appears twice -- once in
the bytes we write, once in a register, because the block builds the slice
header itself -- and the two have to agree. They did not: the SPS declared
pic_order_cnt_type 2, which carries no picture order in the slice header,
while the block emits pic_order_cnt_lsb regardless. A decoder then read
every later field at the wrong bit offset, which surfaced as a corrupt first
macroblock rather than as anything resembling a header problem. The SPS now
uses type 0 and both halves are driven from the same fields.

Two more faults were found on hardware and are worth recording:

  - The motion-search thumbnail was sized with a truncating divide, so the
    last partial row of 64x64 blocks was not allocated and the block wrote
    past the end. At small pictures the 8K rounding hid it; at 1280x720 the
    IOMMU faulted exactly one byte past the buffer.

  - A client that drains -- which is every client at end of stream -- waits
    for V4L2_BUF_FLAG_LAST on the final capture buffer. Without it the
    hardware produced a perfectly good frame that nobody ever collected.

A software watchdog bounds each job. Whichever of the interrupt and the
timeout claims the job finishes it, so the two can never both complete it,
and the block is reset before its buffers are handed back. Before this, a
job that never raised its interrupt took the machine down with it.

Register offsets, bit positions and the tuning tables are hardware facts
with no published derivation; their provenance is recorded in the files
that carry them.

Verified on an Orange Pi 5B: 30 frames of 1280x720 NV12 encode to 1014122
bytes, decode back as 30 frames with no errors, and measure 43.8 dB PSNR
against the source.

Signed-off-by: Ubuntu Rockchip Builder <builder@localhost>
---
diff -uNr -x '.*' -x '*.o' -x '*.ko' -x '*.mod' -x '*.mod.c' -x modules.order -x Module.symvers -x Kconfig base/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile ktest/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/Makefile
